China Probing with Bayonets

There is a maxim, once attributed to Lenin, that still echoes in the corridors of power: “Probe with bayonets. If you encounter steel, withdraw. If you encounter mush, march on.”

It is as much a political doctrine as a military one. And nowhere today is it practiced with greater precision than in Beijing.

China has perfected the art of the tactical probe: push, escalate, destabilize—then retreat just long enough to extract concessions with fresh threats. 

The pattern is clear in the South China Sea, where Chinese vessels harass the Philippines in Scarborough Shoal. #Beijing provokes, then rewrites the narrative, painting itself as victim while others quietly concede ground.

But this doctrine is not confined to #Asia.

The arteries of global commerce—our shipping lanes—are themselves under pressure. 

Houthi attacks in the Red Sea have already forced container ships to bypass the Suez Canal, rerouting thousands of miles around Africa. Each diversion costs millions in money, time, and security. 

Washington recognized the stakes, launching Operation Prosperity Guardian in December 2023 to safeguard one of the world’s most vital trade routes.

Since late 2023, #Houthi forces in #Yemen have carried out an estimated 100 to 200 attacks on shipping in the Red Sea and Gulf of Aden. 

The higher figure, closer to 190, includes incidents targeting both military and commercial vessels. 

Since the launch of Operation Prosperity Guardian in December 2023, roughly 75 attacks have specifically targeted commercial shipping.

Operation Prosperity Guardian is a U.S.-led multinational effort to safeguard maritime traffic through one of the world’s busiest trade corridors. 

