Now cast your eyes to the Horn of Africa.

Somaliland, unrecognized yet resiliently democratic, sits at the mouth of the Gulf of #Aden—an entry point to global trade as strategic as the #Suez itself. 

Beijing knows this. 

For years, it has labored to suffocate Somaliland diplomatically: leaning on @_AfricanUnion states, pressuring Western envoys, even attempting to bribe #Somaliland officials to abandon ties with #Taiwan. 

Meanwhile, Chinese state-owned companies are circling Berbera Port like vultures, eager to bring it into Beijing’s expanding maritime strategy.

The warning signs are unmistakable. 

Djibouti—Somaliland’s neighbor—already hosts China’s first overseas military base. Economic leverage, then political pressure, then armed presence: the formula is tested and proven.

As Mogadishu maneuvers to assert influence over Somaliland’s eastern territories, Beijing’s involvement adds a new dimension—supporting what amounts to an insurgency challenging Somaliland’s pursuit of independence.

A local dispute, yes, but one that fits seamlessly into China’s doctrine of disruption: weaken the periphery, divide the allies, and seize advantage while the West debates.

The lesson is stark. China tests the world exactly as Lenin prescribed: probing where resistance is weak, retreating only when it meets unyielding steel.

And the steel required in Somaliland is recognition.

For Washington and London, anything less will look like mush.

And mush, as Lenin warned, only invites the bayonet.
